(1649, 2036, 'Alex Garland', 'alex-garland', 'https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/h632/qSWzYnDw68xm4i5xprH9BwQ6q25.jpg', 'Alexander Medawar Garland (born 26 May 1970) is an English author, screenwriter, and director. He rose to prominence with his novel The Beach (1996). He received praise for writing the Danny Boyle films 28 Days Later (2002) and Sunshine (2007), as well as Never Let Me Go (2010) and Dredd (2012). In video games, he co-wrote Enslaved: Odyssey to the West (2010) and was a story supervisor on DmC: Devil May Cry (2013).\n\nGarland made his directorial debut when he wrote and directed the sci-fi thriller Ex Machina (2014). He earned an Academy Award nomination for Best Original Screenplay. He won three British Independent Film Awards, including Best Screenplay, Best Director, and Best British Independent Film for the film. His second movie, Annihilation (2018), an adaptation of the 2014 novel of the same name, was a critical success. He wrote, directed, and executive produced the FX miniseries Devs (2020), followed by the horror thriller Men (2022) and the dystopian action thriller Civil War (2024). He also co-directed the war film Warfare (2025). A24 produced the three films.\n\nDescription above from the Wikipedia article Alex Garland, licensed under CC-BY-SA, full list of contributors on Wikipedia.', 'Alexander Medawar Garland (born 26 May 1970) is an English author, screenwriter, and director. He rose to prominence with his novel The Beach (1996). He received praise for writing the Danny Boyle films 28 Days Later (2002) and Sunshine (2007), as well as Never Let Me Go (2010) and Dredd (2012). In video games, he co-wrote Enslaved: Odyssey to the West (2010) and was a story supervisor on DmC: Devil May Cry (2013).\n\nGarland made his directorial debut when he wrote and directed the sci-fi thriller Ex Machina (2014). He earned an Academy Award nomination for Best Original Screenplay. He won three British Independent Film Awards, including Best Screenplay, Best Director, and Best British Independent Film for the film. His second movie, Annihilation (2018), an adaptation of the 2014 novel of the same name, was a critical success. He wrote, directed, and executive produced the FX miniseries Devs (2020), followed by the horror thriller Men (2022) and the dystopian action thriller Civil War (2024). He also co-directed the war film Warfare (2025). A24 produced the three films.\n\nDescription above from the Wikipedia article Alex Garland, licensed under CC-BY-SA, full list of contributors on Wikipedia.', NULL, '1970-05-26', NULL, 'UK', 'Writing', 2, 'London, England, UK', '[\"Alexander Medawar Garland\",\"Alexander Garland\"]', NULL, 'nm0307497', NULL, NULL, '2026-05-08 13:53:48', '2026-05-08 13:53:48', NULL),
(1650, 2034, 'Danny Boyle', 'danny-boyle', 'https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/h632/b5qQpFHmgNyvV1t81ou17Jt2fRj.jpg', 'Daniel Francis Boyle (born 20 October 1956) is an English filmmaker and producer. He is best known for his work on films such as Trainspotting, 28 Days Later, Slumdog Millionaire, 127 Hours, and Steve Jobs. In 2009, Slumdog Millionaire won Boyle the Academy Award for Best Director, and the film won Best Picture. In 2012, Boyle was the artistic director for Isles of Wonder, the opening ceremony of the Summer Olympics in London, which was widely praised.', 'Daniel Francis Boyle (born 20 October 1956) is an English filmmaker and producer. He is best known for his work on films such as Trainspotting, 28 Days Later, Slumdog Millionaire, 127 Hours, and Steve Jobs. In 2009, Slumdog Millionaire won Boyle the Academy Award for Best Director, and the film won Best Picture. In 2012, Boyle was the artistic director for Isles of Wonder, the opening ceremony of the Summer Olympics in London, which was widely praised.', NULL, '1956-10-20', NULL, 'UK', 'Directing', 2, 'Manchester, England, UK', '[\"\\u4e39\\u5c3c\\u00b7\\u9b91\\u4f0a\",\"\\u0e41\\u0e14\\u0e19\\u0e19\\u0e35 \\u0e1a\\u0e2d\\u0e22\\u0e25\\u0e4c\",\"\\ub300\\ub2c8 \\ubcf4\\uc77c\",\"\\u062f\\u0627\\u0646\\u064a \\u0628\\u0648\\u064a\\u0644\",\"\\u039d\\u03c4\\u03ac\\u03bd\\u03b9 \\u039c\\u03c0\\u03cc\\u03b9\\u03bb\",\"Daniel Francis Boyle\",\"\\u0921\\u0948\\u0928\\u0940 \\u092c\\u0949\\u092f\\u0932\",\"\\u0ca1\\u0ccd\\u0caf\\u0cbe\\u0ca8\\u0cbf \\u0cac\\u0ccb\\u0caf\\u0ccd\\u0cb2\\u0cc6\",\"\\u0b9f\\u0bc7\\u0ba9\\u0bbf \\u0baa\\u0bbe\\u0baf\\u0bbf\\u0bb2\\u0bcd\",\"\\u0c21\\u0c3e\\u0c28\\u0c40 \\u0c2c\\u0c3e\\u0c2f\\u0c3f\\u0c32\\u0c4d\",\"\\u0e41\\u0e14\\u0e19\\u0e19\\u0e35\\u0e48\\u0e1a\\u0e2d\\u0e22\\u0e25\\u0e4c\",\"\\u0688\\u06cc\\u0646\\u06cc \\u0628\\u0648\\u0627\\u0626\\u0644\",\"\\u0921\\u094d\\u092f\\u093e\\u0928\\u0940 \\u092c\\u094b\\u092f\\u0932\",\"\\u0aa1\\u0ac7\\u0aa8\\u0ac0 \\u0aac\\u0acb\\u0a87\\u0ab2\"]', NULL, 'nm0000965', NULL, NULL, '2026-05-08 13:53:50', '2026-05-08 13:53:50', NULL),
(1651, 1795072, 'Nia DaCosta', 'nia-dacosta', 'https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/h632/zRyaLNXLT2gKgPvLreyg3GeEIdD.jpg', 'Nia DaCosta (born November 8, 1989) is an American filmmaker. She rose to prominence with her feature-length directorial debut, Little Woods (2018), a crime thriller, which won the Nora Ephron Prize for Female Filmmakers at the Tribeca Film Festival.\n\nAfter working on other projects, most notably directing two episodes of the British thriller series Top Boy in 2019, DaCosta became the first Black female director to debut at No. 1 at the U.S. box office for the weekend opening of the horror film Candyman (2021). She then became the first black woman to direct a Marvel Comics film with The Marvels (2023), which, despite being a box-office bomb, became the highest-grossing film directed by a black woman.\n\nDescription above from the Wikipedia article Nia DaCosta, licensed under CC-BY-SA, full list of contributors on Wikipedia.', 'Nia DaCosta (born November 8, 1989) is an American filmmaker. She rose to prominence with her feature-length directorial debut, Little Woods (2018), a crime thriller, which won the Nora Ephron Prize for Female Filmmakers at the Tribeca Film Festival.\n\nAfter working on other projects, most notably directing two episodes of the British thriller series Top Boy in 2019, DaCosta became the first Black female director to debut at No. 1 at the U.S. box office for the weekend opening of the horror film Candyman (2021). She then became the first black woman to direct a Marvel Comics film with The Marvels (2023), which, despite being a box-office bomb, became the highest-grossing film directed by a black woman.\n\nDescription above from the Wikipedia article Nia DaCosta, licensed under CC-BY-SA, full list of contributors on Wikipedia.', NULL, '1989-11-08', NULL, 'USA', 'Directing', 1, 'Brooklyn, New York City, New York, USA', NULL, NULL, 'nm4804442', NULL, '', '2026-05-08 13:53:52', '2026-05-08 13:53:52', NULL),
(1652, 2035, 'Andrew Macdonald', 'andrew-macdonald', 'https://image.tmdb.org/t/p/h632/sDVEoOK4mLZpfxhxP6y5figCdsf.jpg', 'Andrew Macdonald (born January 1966) is a Scottish film producer best known for his collaborations with director Danny Boyle on films including Shallow Grave (1994), Trainspotting (1996), The Beach (2000) and 28 Days Later (2002). He has also frequently collaborated with screenwriter John Hodge and writer-director Alex Garland.\n\nDescription above from the Wikipedia article, Andrew Macdonald (producer), licensed under CC-BY-SA, full list of contributors on Wikipedia.', 'Andrew Macdonald (born January 1966) is a Scottish film producer best known for his collaborations with director Danny Boyle on films including Shallow Grave (1994), Trainspotting (1996), The Beach (2000) and 28 Days Later (2002). He has also frequently collaborated with screenwriter John Hodge and writer-director Alex Garland.\n\nDescription above from the Wikipedia article, Andrew Macdonald (producer), licensed under CC-BY-SA, full list of contributors on Wikipedia.', NULL, '1966-01-01', NULL, 'UK', 'Production', 2, 'Glasgow, Scotland, UK', NULL, NULL, 'nm0531602', NULL, NULL, '2026-05-08 13:53:54', '2026-05-08 13:53:54', NULL),
